// const CACHE_NAME  = 'workhive-shell-v417';  // bump 2026-09-16 (83rd): utils.js (precached SHELL) - C28's SECOND HALF: THE UPDATE NOTICE SAT ON TOP OF EVERY PANEL A READER OPENED, AND THE LEDGER SAID THE DEFECT WAS FIXED. C28 was recorded 2026-09-15 and marked "fixed (2026-09-15) - verified live through the Playwright MCP on the shipped shell (sw v394)". That verification was real and it covered the AT-REST half: the notice now raises itself above the page-guide chip (data-wh-bottom 203px). But the entry's own `improvement` line says the notice must measure "the fixed bottom chrome already on the page (learn chip, sticky CTA, hub fab, OPEN PANELS)", and the open-panel half was never implemented - _placeNotice's avoidance list is '#wh-hub, .wh-conn-chip, .wh-conn-popover, .wh-fb-fab, #wh-guide-link, #wh-ai-widget, #fab, .wh-companion-trigger', every entry a BUTTON or a chip. `.wh-fb-fab` is the feedback LAUNCHER; the panel it opens is not in the list, and neither is the companion's message area. So the notice kept clear of the controls that summon the panels and sat on top of the panels themselves. A "fixed" status is what stops anyone looking again, so C28 is back to open (half-fixed) with both halves named. WHY PLACEMENT COULD NEVER FIX IT, measured live at phone-390 on the shipped shell before the change: #wh-update-notice at 532..643 (z 2147483000, fixed), #wh-feedback-panel at 0..844 - the FULL viewport - (z 9999, fixed), #wh-fb-body at 522..622, overlaps true. To clear an 844px panel the notice would need bottom >= 844, past the 60% cap (506px) that placement deliberately refuses to cross so a stacked page cannot push the message out of sight. The cap is correct and the z-index is correct too: a transport-level "your version is stale" message must outrank page CONTENT. What it must not outrank is a panel the reader deliberately opened one second ago. So this is a Z-ORDER problem and placement is simply not the mechanism for it. THE FIX: while a visible overlay INTERSECTS the notice, the notice hides; when the overlay closes it returns, with its remaining lifetime unchanged. Three things keep it narrow. It tests INTERSECTION rather than mere presence, so a popover elsewhere on screen does not silence the message. The overlay set is the platform's own - [aria-modal="true"], [role="dialog"], .sheet.open, #wh-hub-panel, #wh-ai-panel, #wh-feedback-panel - plus `.wh-fb-panel.open`, which is the class the feedback panel actually carries (measured live, not assumed). And display:none / visibility:hidden / zero-opacity / under-8px overlays are ignored, because a CLOSED off-canvas panel keeps its box and this platform has already been caught reading one as open. The re-check runs on a 500ms interval created with the notice and cleared by the same 30s cleanup that removes it, so no page carries a timer for a message that is gone; a MutationObserver over the body subtree was the alternative and would have made 157 pages pay attribute traffic for something that lives half a minute. The notice is never DISMISSED by this, only hidden while something covers it: the reader's own dismissal still records in _whNoticeDismissed and the 30s self-removal is untouched. HOW THIS SURFACED, AND THE GATE CHANGE THAT MADE IT POSSIBLE: bank_mcp_navhub_walk.py summed findings from <id>.json - the 4 page ARRIVALS - and never opened <id>.records.json, the ~24 INTERACTION records where the hub, companion and feedback panel are actually opened. A page at rest is the one state where a panel cannot be covering anything, because it is not open yet, so the gate was measuring the only state its own sentence - "no control occluded at any step" - does not depend on. A fresh MCP walk of dayplanner returned 2 occlusions and the banker said "findings 0". It now reads the records file and REFUSES to bank a nav-hub row while an interactive state carries an occlusion; of the 33 banked rows, 2 (W41367, W41368) carry a finding that was never counted, both the same "feedback open from the hub".  // bump 2026-09-16 (82nd): index.html (precached SHELL) - THE FRONT DOOR CARRIED THREE CONSTRUCTS ITS OWN DESIGN RECORD FORBIDS IN ONE SENTENCE, and the detector had been counting them since the design sweep began. DESIGN.md:167 reads "Don't: micro-labels under 12 px; all-caps tracked eyebrows as headings; kickers above headings; nested cards; zero-offset glows; continuous decorative animation; gradient text; ..." - so none of this needed a judgement call, which is the standard set after I once pinned 183 detector findings as "the platform's consistent vocabulary" while DESIGN.md forbade the construct in one line. (1) FIVE KICKERS DELETED. "In one paragraph" above "What is WorkHive?", "How the hive works" above "Four gaps. One hive.", "The hive in action" above "The day after WorkHive.", "Find your role" above "Every worker has a role in the hive.", "Free guides" above "Practical writing for the Philippine plant floor". The craft floor states this as the one item on its Refuse list that is not a default: "A kicker or eyebrow above a heading. This one is a ban, not a default: no brief earns it back. The heading carries its own weight; delete the label and let the heading speak." Each was `text-xs ... uppercase tracking-[0.15em]`, so each also carried three MORE things the same DESIGN.md sentence forbids - a micro-label under 12px, all-caps body text, and tracked-out eyebrow lettering. Deleting the element removes all of them at once: the fix for a construct the record bans is its absence, not a smaller version. No heading was touched and no copy rewritten. (2) EIGHT GRADIENT-TEXT SITES REPLACED WITH THE HUE'S -text TOKEN - the .stat-orange and .stat-blue rules (which carry the big numbers 40-60%, 4 Gaps, 60-80% in nine places) and six inline spans ("Memory", "hive's stage", "One hive.", "WorkHive.", "has a role in the hive.", "Build the future."). The solid is not a guess: index.html's own comment already carried the rule - the darkest stop of a background-clip:text gradient IS the text, so it takes the -text token - and that comment records the 2026-08-06 measurement behind it (.stat-blue at 48px on the #impact card measured APCA Lc 44.6 against a floor of 45, because the gradient painted its own darkest stop while the declared color:#fff was never painted; --wh-blue-text #5FCCE8 measures 56.8). The accent survives as colour, which the record permits everywhere; what goes is the gradient, and font-black still carries the weight the floor says emphasis should come from. THE EIGHTH SITE WAS FOUND BY THE DETECTOR, NOT BY ME: my census grep was piped through `head -8`, so the list I worked from was the list I had LOOKED at rather than the list that exists - a glob that describes where I searched, for the third time this wave. The re-census is `grep -c background-clip index.html` returning 1, that one being the comment. (3) FOUR ACCENT BORDERS THINNED 3px -> 1px on the stage cards, against DESIGN.md:149 "accent borders are 1 px TINTS of the status hue, never a thick coloured border-left" - the rule is about the accent, not about which edge carries it, and this is the same correction applied to dayplanner's two schedule-block accents in v415. The hue stays at full strength so a reader still sees which stage a card is; only the slab goes. (4) THE STAT-NUMBER COMMENT REWRITTEN, because the last surviving gradient-text finding WAS that comment: the detector reads a stylesheet as text, so a comment describing `background-clip:text GRADIENT` scans identically to the construct - the same class as grepping a commented-out CACHE_NAME instead of the active one. It needed rewriting anyway, since it opened "stat number gradient" above two rules that no longer contain a gradient and would have told the next reader the page does something it stopped doing. The whole 2026-08-06 APCA measurement is KEPT: it is the reason the -text tokens are the right SOLID, not merely the right gradient stop, and without it `color: var(--wh-blue-text)` reads as an arbitrary preference a later edit could "simplify" back to the failing hue. MEASURED: index.html primary detector findings 44 -> 30 (kicker-above-heading 5 -> 0, gradient-text 5 -> 0, border-accent-on-rounded 4 -> 0, and the all-caps-body / wide-tracking the kickers carried). Remaining and NOT touched in this bump because each needs per-element judgement and a live read at both viewports: clipped-overflow-container 9, nested-cards 8, dark-glow 7, plus one each of wide-tracking, all-caps-body, cramped-padding, radial-halo, marketing-buzzword, aphoristic-cadence. ALSO IN THIS BUMP: the platform ratchet's floor fell 368 -> 246 because it had been counting 122 findings that describe a page nobody can see - `@media print { body * { color:#111 } }` paints every element black FOR PAPER against a #fff set in the same block, and the static detector measured that colour against the SCREEN background, reporting 1.1:1 on text that is 17.9:1 where it actually renders. It turned into a false failure when ONE added <option> - the fix for a real defect where the logbook's discipline filter silently hid 7,987 entries - took logbook low-contrast 92 -> 93 and the gate refused the change. A colour is now exempt only when it appears inside an @media print block and NOWHERE outside one, checked per file, because suppressing a whole class on the strength of part of it being an artifact is the over-correction this wave already learned to refuse.  // bump 2026-09-16 (81st): dayplanner.html + logbook.html (both precached SHELL) - FOUR DEFECTS THE ISOLATED PAIR FOUND IN ROUND 2, PLUS THE ONE A GATE FOUND BY ASKING THE DATABASE A QUESTION THE PRODUCT NEVER ASKED. (1) A DELETE REPORTED SUCCESS WHEN IT DELETED NOTHING. deleteItemFromSupabase ran .delete().eq('id',id).eq('worker_name',WORKER_NAME) with no .select(), and PostgREST answers a DELETE that matched no row with 204 No Content and error === null - so an RLS refusal and a worker_name mismatch were both byte-identical to success. The caller had already removed the item locally, so the reader watched it vanish, was told nothing was wrong, and the row stayed in the database and came back on the next load. The existing error branch was correct and simply never ran for this case. Now .select('id'), and a zero-row result restores the item exactly as a refusal does: of the two possible wrong answers that is the safe one, because if the row really had been deleted in another tab the next load corrects the restore and nothing is lost, whereas a false success loses a delete the person believes they made. (2) THE LAST LINE OF openEditModal UNDID THE FIX WRITTEN ABOVE IT. That function sets currentModalStatus = item.itemStatus || null under a long comment explaining why a DERIVED status must never be pre-loaded into a control that gets saved - getItemStatus returns 'missed' for any past-dated row nobody has judged, which maps to the canonical 'skipped', so editing ONLY a title silently recorded a compliance judgement. Its final line then called setItemStatus(effStatus) to highlight the right button, and setItemStatus's FIRST statement is an assignment to currentModalStatus. The derived value went straight back into the variable the fix had just cleared, and the defect survived inside the fix that describes it - the same shape as the locale fix whose own comment asserted a bilingual page it could not produce. Painting a control and CHOOSING its value are two acts: _dpPaintStatusButtons only paints, setItemStatus keeps both jobs for the three onclick handlers where a person really is choosing, and openEditModal now calls only the painter. (3) A SAVE CONFLICT ASKED THE PERSON TO REDO WORK THE PAGE HAD JUST DESTROYED. The optimistic-lock branch said "Showing you their version - make your change again" and then ran loadSchedule(), which replaces scheduleItems with the server's rows - so after it ran the words the person typed existed NOWHERE: the UPDATE matched no row, and the reload overwrote the local copy that held them. The editor was already closed too, because syncItemToSupabase is called un-awaited at the save site, so "again" meant retyping from memory. Losing a conflicting WRITE is right; losing the TEXT is not. The attempted row is kept, the reload puts the other person's version underneath where it belongs, and _dpReopenWithAttempt puts the draft back into the editor so one more Save applies it - and when the other person DELETED the item there is nothing to re-open, so the helper reports that and the sentence says so rather than promising an editor that will not appear. (4) THE LOGBOOK'S DISCIPLINE FILTER SILENTLY HID A QUARTER OF THE BOOK. 7,987 of 31,240 rows (25.6%) carry category IS NULL - five years of CMMS-imported history, where the source work order has no WorkHive discipline to give, which is honest. The filter offered "All categories" and seven disciplines and no way to ask for the entries that have none, and both matchers are exact-equality (client :4276, server :1931), so choosing any discipline dropped all 7,987 with nothing on screen to say so. A ninth option, "No discipline recorded" (value __none), reaches them; the team-view query needs .is('category', null) because .eq(...) on a null never matches; _logSyncUrl needed no change because __none is a real option value and round-trips through ?cat= on its own. Nothing about the other eight choices was wrong and no imported row was given an invented discipline. ALSO IN THIS BUMP, NOT A PAGE CHANGE BUT THE REASON THREE OF THESE WERE FOUND: tools/prove_capture_enums_cover_the_data.py ran live for the first time and found FIVE contract enums that omit values their column holds - then reported all five as outages, which was true of two. validates_at is a COLUMN, not a mechanism (no edge function reads canonical_capture_contracts at all); a contract governs ONE surface's writes while the check compares the WHOLE column; and of the four captures a page actually validates, only schedule_item_v1 re-sends rows it LOADED - which is why it is the only one that has ever blocked a save, twice. The gate now sorts every mismatch into blocking / not-reached / latent / unannotated, counts NULLs with IS NULL instead of = ANY() (which matches nothing, so the logbook finding had printed no blast radius at all), and its self-test fails when an enforced capture is unannotated. Migrations 20260916000001-3 are written and await Ian: simulated against live data they take the blocking class from 2 to 0.  // bump 2026-09-16 (80th): dayplanner.html (precached SHELL) - MY OWN ROUND-1 LOCALISATION FIX WAS INERT, AND THE COMMENT I WROTE ASSERTED IT WORKED. Found by the round-2 critique pair (W45908: A 23/40, B 21/40, so 21 is the score of record and the row stays open). In round 1 I localised DAY_NAMES through Intl to match MONTH_NAMES beside it, and wrote a note saying this ended the case where "the month view rendered 'Setyembre 2026' above columns headed Sun Mon Tue Wed Thu Fri Sat". Both are top-level IIFEs that read `window.WH_LANG`. MEASURED IN THE FILE: they evaluate at character 87,969; the utils.js tag - the ONLY thing on this page that assigns WH_LANG - is at character 195,563; nothing sets it earlier and wh-i18n-lite.js is not loaded here at all. So the condition was evaluated roughly 108,000 characters before the value it tests existed, and resolved en-PH for every reader, always. PROVEN IN NODE with the real expression before and after: eager -> "September", lazy -> "Setyembre" (the runtime does have fil-PH). Round 1 therefore did not make the page bilingual here - it made it CONSISTENTLY English instead of inconsistently English, and left a comment asserting the opposite. That is precisely the defect round 1 itself found on this page (a guard whose long comment explains why it is essential and which can never execute) recurring inside the fix written for it. whFmtDate in utils.js was right all along because it reads WH_LANG INSIDE the function, which is why the period labels were Filipino while the month and weekday names were not - two paths disagreeing inside one view. THE FIX: evaluated on first USE and memoised, with Proxy objects so the ~20 existing MONTH_NAMES[m] / DAY_NAMES[d] call sites need no change and none can be missed. Verified on the shipped file, not on a diff: en -> "September | Sun Mon Tue", fil -> "Setyembre | Lin Lun Mar". ALSO IN THIS BUMP, from the same batch: dayplanner's two schedule-block accents go 3px -> 1px, because DESIGN.md's Shapes section says "accent borders are 1 px tints of the status hue, NEVER a thick coloured border-left" - a rule I had earlier pinned 183 findings AGAINST, arguing from how consistently the code did it, which is what a drift looks like from the inside. Ratchet holds at 368 primary findings (943 at the start of the design sweep). The page walks clean: nav-hub phone-390 en, 24 records, 11/11 hub controls, 0 findings.  // bump 2026-09-16 (79th): utils.js, asset-hub.html, dayplanner.html (all precached SHELL) - THE THICK COLOURED LEFT BAR, WHICH I HAD PINNED AS "OUR VOCABULARY" AND DESIGN.md FORBIDS IN ONE SENTENCE. This bump is a correction to my own work earlier today. I pinned 183 `side-tab` findings across five files as a false positive, arguing they were "the platform's consistent vocabulary for a notice" - reasoning from what the code does. That is backwards, and the craft floor had already said so: "A pinned brief or the committed visual world overrides anything here." The committed visual world is DESIGN.md, and its Shapes section decides the question outright: "Hairlines `1px solid rgba(255,255,255,0.07-0.10)`; accent borders are 1 px tints of the status hue, NEVER a thick coloured `border-left`." Impeccable's craft floor refuses the same construct from the other side - "a colored border-left or border-right ABOVE 1px on cards, list items, callouts, or alerts". The detector was right on every one of them and I had told the next session the question was settled. A wrong pin is worse than an open finding. All five pins are retracted in their .disproven.json files with the reason recorded, and the construct is fixed: the learn articles' `.callout` and `.answer-first` (96 borders across 54 files) become a 1px tint of the same hue on all four sides, which is the pattern DESIGN.md prescribes and the platform's own chip component already uses; utils.js's four shared strip builders and its two card builders, asset-hub's four (two at 3px and two at 2px - the rule says ABOVE 1px, so 2px is in scope too) and dayplanner's two schedule-block accents go to 1px, which keeps the status hue doing its job and drops only the thickness the rules object to. ONE EXCEPTION, kept on the rules' own words rather than on how common it is: `.prose-wh blockquote` on the 60 calculator pages is re-pinned, because both rules name the elements they govern and both name the same four - cards, list items, callouts, alerts - and a pull quote is none of them, has no border-radius, sits on no card and is not interactive. A left rule on a blockquote is a typographic convention centuries older than the web. Swinging from a self-serving pin to an over-correction would be the same failure wearing the other sign, so the test applied was what the rule NAMES, not what the detector labels. The learn-callout tool is selector-aware for a measured reason: `.answer-first` and `.prose-wh blockquote` carry the BYTE-IDENTICAL declaration `border-left: 3px solid #F7A21B`, so a plain string replace would have rewritten the one construct that was meant to survive - its self-test asserts exactly that separation in a file holding both. Ratchet 546 -> 428. And the 30-host nav-hub walk finished 30/30 with zero findings, which is the live proof that the 143-file type-floor sweep in v410 broke no layout: every control exercised, no control occluded at any step, focus returned.  // bump 2026-09-16 (78th): hive.html, marketplace.html, marketplace-seller.html, report-sender.html, resume.html, founder-console.html + 15 more (precached SHELL among them) - the type floor raised to the PLATFORM's bar, and the retired bounce curve removed from the five files that still spelled it out. TWO CORRECTIONS TO MY OWN WORK, both caught by instruments rather than by review. (1) The first type-floor sweep used 11.5px, which is the DETECTOR's functional-text bar, not the platform's. That left 32 `tiny-text` findings standing at 11.52px and 11.84px - sizes that clear the 11px functional floor and fail the 12px BODY bar, which is the floor this platform actually sets for itself. Raised to 11.95 (not a bare 12, which rounds 0.75rem in on some values) and swept again: 172 more declarations across 21 files, marketplace.html 34, founder-console.html 26, marketplace-seller.html 21, hive.html 16. The print exemption grew with it, 14 -> 25 declarations, and is still REPORTED rather than applied silently. (2) THE RETIRED SPRING OUTLIVED ITS RETIREMENT IN FIVE MORE FILES. tokens.css retired --wh-ease-spring - cubic-bezier(0.34, 1.56, 0.64, 1) - for overshooting by 56%, and the git grep that confirmed it was unused searched the token's NAME. resume.html, marketplace.html, marketplace-seller.html, marketplace-seller-profile.html and report-sender.html each hard-code the identical curve as a LITERAL on their toast, so the platform's one dated bounce survived the decision that removed it in every file that had not been walked yet. All five are toasts, a toast entering is an entrance, and review-animations/STANDARDS.md gives entrances ease-out at the platform's own duration step - the same fix and the same reasoning already recorded on integrations.html. AND A THIRD CORRECTION, to the INSTRUMENT: my inline-script syntax checker reported resume.html and report-sender.html as broken after these edits, and they were already failing identically at HEAD - it was extracting a commented-out <script> from inside `<!-- ... -->` and parsing the prose around it ("block runs -->") as JavaScript. I read that as my own edit having broken two pages and only disproved it by re-running the checker against `git show HEAD:<file>`. It now strips HTML comments before extracting, because a verification instrument that cries wolf cannot answer the one question it exists for. Ratchet 584 -> 546; all 21 edited pages re-parse.  // bump 2026-09-16 (77th): marketplace-seller.html (precached SHELL) - the ONE real low-contrast defect on the platform, found by resolving the other 136 to their mechanisms. A census of all 137 `low-contrast` findings: 122 are `@media print`'s #111 read against screen navy (logbook 92, pm-scheduler 30, both pinned disproven), 8 are analytics-report's dark app shell paired with its own white printed document (already pinned), and 6 more are three distinct static-reader blind spots now pinned with their proofs. public-feed: `.header-btn.secondary` overrides its base's amber gradient with `background: transparent`, which the detector does not resolve to the ancestor's navy - and it cannot tell the two amber tokens apart anyway, because tokens.css defines BOTH `--wh-orange-light` (line 63, a fill) and `--wh-orange-text` (line 110, the AA-safe text amber) as #FDB94A, so it pairs the modifier's text with the base's background and reports 1.0:1 where the truth is 9.49:1. project-report: the Print button carries an INLINE amber gradient beside its inline navy text, and the detector keeps the STYLESHEET background (`.toolbar button{background:#1d2e44}` and its #243a55 hover) - the same class arriving through the other door, a modifier there, an inline style here. feedback/index: the hero heading is `-webkit-background-clip:text` with `-webkit-text-fill-color:transparent`, so NEITHER reported colour paints anything - `#ffffff on #ffffff` is the inherited colour against a white that does not exist on this dark page, and the amber pair is the gradient's second stop; what renders is the gradient on navy. THAT LEFT EXACTLY ONE, and it was real: `