// const CACHE_NAME  = 'workhive-shell-v348';  // bump 2026-09-10 (W3-JN, the offline-3g cluster's ROOT): ONE LOST REQUEST OUT OF 65 DESTROYED THE ENTIRE PWA INSTALL, AND NOTHING SAID SO. install ran `cache.addAll(SHELL_FILES)`, which is ATOMIC - any single failed entry rejects, the rejection escapes waitUntil, and the worker goes from `installing` straight to `redundant`. The person is then left with NO worker at all: no precache, no offline fallback, no network-first copy of the page they were on, and no message anywhere. Measured in a browser rather than argued: registering under the journey prover's own offline-3g emulation returned `registered:true, state:installing, finalState:REDUNDANT` with sw.js itself never among the dropped requests, while the same page with routing present but nothing dropped installed and activated normally - so the LOSS killed it, not the instrumentation. The arithmetic is the argument and it is not only about a stress test: the install survives only if all 65 entries land, so at offline-3g's 1-in-12 that is (11/12)^65 = 0.35%, at an ordinary 1% request loss it is (0.99)^65 = 52% - HALF of real plant-floor installs failing completely - and at 3% it is 14%. Each file now gets ONE retry (a dropped request is usually unlucky rather than unavailable, the same reasoning browser-floor.js applies to a dropped script) and a file that still will not come is left out rather than taking the install down with it; a worker holding 63 of 65 files is a working shell, a worker that never activates is nothing, and the fetch handler already falls back to the network and then the offline shell for anything absent. Verified both ways after the change: WITH drops -> activated, 65/65 cached (the retry recovered every one); without drops -> activated, 65/65. THIS IS ALSO WHY THE OFFLINE-3G CLUSTER LOOKED THE WAY IT DID, and the correction is owed to every claim made about it: under that condition the service worker NEVER installed, so every offline-3g journey has been measuring a platform whose worker was inert - the precache, the _appNav navigation copy and the _appScript copy were all absent, and the v345 note's "a cold first visit whose navigation is the dropped request is not recoverable on the client at all" was reasoning about a worker that was not running. Those rows are being re-walked against a platform that now actually has one. AND ONE CONSEQUENCE OF THIS FIX, HANDLED IN THE SAME BUMP because it is caused by it: a PARTIAL shell is now possible for the first time, so the offline fallback itself may be the file that was left out - and the old `new Response('', {status: 503})` would then paint a person a WHITE PAGE, which tells them less than the browser's own "no internet" screen. Both fallback sites now return Response.error() when the shell is not cached, handing the navigation back to the browser instead of inventing a blank one. Before this turn that branch was unreachable - the atomic install meant the shell was either wholly cached or the worker never activated at all.
// const CACHE_NAME  = 'workhive-shell-v347';  // bump 2026-09-10 (W3-JN, sw-offline L5): A SCRIPT 38 PAGES DEPEND ON COULD BE SERVED BY NO ROUTE. utils.js is 363KB, is loaded by 38 pages, and defines getDb - every database call on the platform goes through it - and it is in no SHELL_FILES entry, while wh-help.js (10KB, ONE page) and form-autosave.js (6KB, three) are precached. companion-launcher.js (99KB, 29 pages) sat in the same place. The old handler only WROTE to the cache for /learn/ and /tools/, so neither could ever enter it by any route: lose one of those requests and the page throws `window.getDb is not a function` with nothing to fall back on, which is W3122's error under offline-3g exactly. Same-origin SCRIPTS that are not already precached now go network-FIRST with a kept copy - the live file whenever the network answers, the last good one when it does not. That is the argument the _appNav branch already makes for app pages, and it applies harder here: precaching these two would bill every install 462KB for files many visitors never open, and cache-first would then serve yesterday's JavaScript until a bump. Scripts ALREADY in SHELL_FILES deliberately stay on the cache-first path - they are in the cache from install, so routing them through the network would trade first-paint speed for a guarantee they already have; membership is compared by path SUFFIX because SHELL_FILES entries are origin-root ('/nav-hub.js') while the local tester serves under /workhive/, a prefix that has broken SW paths here before (verified against all 65 entries in both shapes). Two guards ride along: the offline fallback now answers a NAVIGATION with the branded shell and a SCRIPT with an honest 503, because handing a <script src> an HTML page makes the browser throw a syntax error and puts the real cause - one lost request - nowhere in the message; and ignoreSearch is now navigation-only, since a query on an app page selects a tab the same document reads while a query on a script is a cache-buster, so `utils.js?v=2` must never be answered by a stored `utils.js?v=1` (no script on the platform carries a query today - checked - so this closes the trap before the first one). browser-floor.js's retry stays as the belt to these braces: it re-requests a dropped same-origin script, which covers the DROPPED case and not the OFFLINE one. validate_sw_offline L5 now passes; it was added this turn and mutation-tested (make the handler keep scripts, or precache utils.js, and it quiets in the right amounts).
// const CACHE_NAME  = 'workhive-shell-v346';  // bump 2026-09-10 (W3-JN, T492 "offline-fallback degrades gracefully"): THE OFFLINE PAGE SPOKE FILIPINO ONLY WHILE ONLINE. offline-fallback.html (SHELL) carries 13 data-i labels and its Filipino dictionary has been precached since v326 - but the code that APPLIES them, whI18nApply, lives in utils.js, which is 363KB and in no SHELL_FILES entry. So the one page whose entire purpose is to appear when the network has failed was loading a 363KB script over that failed network in order to translate itself, and a Filipino worker on a dropped plant line got the English shell. The swap now happens in the page, which is what an offline shell has to be able to do: it depends on nothing but itself and a file already in the cache. Not a second i18n engine - all 13 of its keys are p_-prefixed and every one is covered by its own dictionary (checked against the json, not assumed), so it needs no shared vocabulary; and the utils.js tag is gone because the page used it for nothing else (its only other code is addEventListener, getElementById and navigator.onLine, all native). Found by asking which pages load utils.js WITHOUT browser-floor.js armed first - 36 of 38 have the retry ahead of it, and the two that do not are offline-fallback.html and status.html. Bumped because offline-fallback.html is precached: without it an installed client keeps the copy that reaches for a file it cannot have. NOTE, recorded because the same walk raised it and it is NOT fixed here: utils.js is loaded by 38 pages and recoverable by no route (not precached, and the fetch handler keeps no copy of same-origin scripts), which is now a standing WARN - validate_sw_offline L5, added this turn with companion-launcher.js beside it. That fix is a fetch-handler change and is deliberately not folded into this bump.
// const CACHE_NAME  = 'workhive-shell-v345';  // bump 2026-09-10 (W3-JN J26 + J25): sw.js ITSELF now answers app-page NAVIGATIONS network-first and keeps a copy, so a worker-critical page survives a dropped request. Measured, not inferred: W3521 (J25, Lucena, offline-3g - a condition that delays every request and ABORTS about one in twelve) reported logbook.html never arriving at all, and W3559 sank the same way on J9. The cause was structural - SHELL_FILES precaches exactly TWO html pages (report-sender, offline-fallback) and the cache-on-visit path was scoped to /learn/ and /tools/, so logbook.html (404KB, the surface a technician opens most) was in the cache by NO route. Precaching it was the wrong fix: hive.html and logbook.html are 434KB and 404KB, that bills every user at install for pages most never open, and cache-FIRST would then serve yesterday's app HTML until the next bump. The offline fallback matches with ignoreSearch, because the copy is stored under the exact URL fetched and a navigation almost never repeats its query - without it logbook.html?tab=history would miss a cached logbook.html and a person would be shown the offline shell while their page sat one character away. Network-first with a kept copy gives the same protection with neither cost, and validate_sw_offline L1 now asks whether the page is RECOVERABLE rather than whether it is precached (mutation-tested: remove the branch and all 7 worker-critical pages redden again). WHAT THIS DOES NOT FIX, said plainly because the measurement that prompted it is not the case it closes: W3521 walked a FRESH context, so its logbook visit was the first, and there was no copy to fall back to - a cold first visit whose navigation is the dropped request is not recoverable on the client at all. This closes the RETURNING visit, which is the common one on a plant floor, and leaves the cold-first-visit-on-a-dropping-line case open and named. ALSO in this bump: browser-floor.js (SHELL) now ASKS ONCE MORE before 